>
  <h2>A whole-home remodel is really a coordination problem.</h2>
  <p>
    Any single room is manageable. What makes a whole-home remodel hard is that every part touches
    every other part — the electrical feeds the kitchen, the kitchen plumbing runs under the new floor,
    the new floor meets the bathroom tile, and the HVAC has to serve all of it. When that coordination
    is handled well, the house comes together as one home. When it isn’t, you end up living inside the
    arguments between trades.
  </p>
  <p>
    That’s why the single most important decision you make isn’t the tile or the cabinet color. It’s who
    owns the whole thing. At Reliable Builders, that’s us — one accountable crew that answers for the
    finished result, with 20 years of hands-on experience across remodels and new builds behind it.
  </p>
  <h3>One team, one number to call</h3>
  <p>
    A lot of homeowners try to save money by hiring trades themselves — a plumber here, an electrician
    there, a tile guy their neighbor recommended. It sounds cheaper until the day the schedules collide,
    something doesn’t line up, and every trade points at the one who came before them. Suddenly you’re
    the project manager of a job you didn’t want to manage.
  </p>
  <p>
    With one crew owning the project, that coordination is our job, not yours. One schedule. One point of
    contact. One group that can’t pass the blame because it’s all theirs to answer for. That’s not a
    sales line — it’s the entire reason a whole-home remodel either goes smoothly or turns into a
    year-long headache.
  </p>
  <h3>Living through it — the honest version</h3>
  <p>
    Most families want to stay in their home if they possibly can, and we’ll always try to make that
    work. On a phased remodel we sequence the zones so you keep a functioning kitchen and at least one
    working bathroom while we build out the rest, then swap as we go. It’s not glamorous — there will be
    plastic sheeting and days you’re tired of it — but it’s livable, and we plan it that way on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a true full gut, we’ll tell you straight that moving out for the heaviest stretch is usually the
    right call. We’d rather be honest about that up front than have you discover it the hard way three
    weeks in. The point is: you’ll know which situation yours is before you plan your life around it.
  </p>
  <h3>The systems you don’t see are the ones that matter</h3>
  <p>
    It’s tempting to spend the whole budget on what’s visible — the counters, the floors, the fixtures.
    But on a whole-home job the real value is in the bones: the electrical, the plumbing, and the HVAC.
    Bringing those up to date is what makes a finished house safe, efficient, and quiet, and it’s
    exactly the work a cut-rate crew skips because you can’t see it. We handle it properly, permit what
    needs permitting, and do the tile and countertop in-house so the everyday surfaces hold up too.
  </p>
  <h3>Older homes always have surprises</h3>
  <p>
    Open up enough walls in a San Diego home and you’ll find something — old wiring, a drain that was
    never right, framing someone improvised decades ago. It happens on almost every whole-home remodel,
    and the difference between a good contractor and a bad one is what happens next. We show you what we
    found, explain your options in plain English, and give you a real number — instead of burying it in
    a vague line item and hoping you don’t ask.
  </p>
  <h3>What your investment gets you</h3>
  <p>
    Whole-home remodels generally start around <strong>$150K</strong> and move from there based on your
    square footage, how many systems we touch, and your finish level. That’s a wide range because a
    whole-house cosmetic refresh and a full structural gut are genuinely different projects. After we
    walk your home and understand what you’re after, we’ll show you exactly where the money goes — and
    where a smart choice saves you real money without you ever feeling it in the finished house.
  </p>
</ServiceLayout>
